// The same rule, on the second transform: `jvp(h)` where `h` is a closure holding `x`.//// `jvp` reads the graph of the function value it is given -- that is the whole transform -- so it needs// the same thing `transpose` needs: a graph it can name at the call. A closure that captures a value// names none (the static function is known, the captured value is not), and the refusal is the same// diagnostic, at the same place, pointing at the same argument:////   error[SemanticDiag::TransformArgumentIsNotAKnownFunction]: 'jvp' is applied to a closure that//   captures a value, and a transform's argument has to be a function this program knows before it runs.//// What the case is here for: the rule is one rule. `transpose`, `jvp` and `split` all declare the same// call-site fact (`CallSpec::staticFunctionArg`), so a shape refused at one of them is refused at all// three, and a reader who learns it once has learned it everywhere. Before the declaration this program// compiled and the lift refused once it ran, in the run-time half's own words.import { jvp } from transformsimport rulesets.jvpfunc by_capture(x: float): float {    let h = (y: float) => y * x    let forward = jvp(h)    let (value, tangent) = forward(2.0, 1.0)    return value + tangent}func main(): int sync {    println(by_capture(3.0))    return 0}